{"id":"700caec5-4392-4e0a-b740-81e5d46ba5be","object":"chat.completion","created":1793334088,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"\n\n```python\nfor p in [\".course/test_logger.py\", \".course/test_logging_config.py\"]:\n    c = read_file(p)[\"content\"]\n    print(\"==\", p, len(c))\n    print(c[:9000])\n```","reasoning_content":"Let me look at environ.py more: the int_or_none descriptor and helpers, and the logging section. Also logger.py init_logger for deepinfer and logger.py rest. Also stats_logger log worker.\n\nI need to check test expectations. Let me read the tests? Tests are in .course/ — allowed to read but not modify. Reading tests would help but reading requirements already precise. Let me read the relevant test files to be careful.\n\nLet me plan implementation:\n\nenviron.py: add descriptor factory e.g.\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(min_value: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)   # empty string raises ValueError\n            if value < min_value:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{value}, must be >= {min_value}\")\n            return value\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\nError text must include the variable name — descriptor adds `{self.name}: {e}` wrapper for exceptions; ValueError is subclass of Exception → wrapped via raise type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\") → ValueError with name. Good.\n\nThen in envs: `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(32)`.\n\nAlso maybe `_int_or_none` helper. Fine.\n\nlogger.py: NewLineFormatter.__init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None). Validate: if max_message_chars is not None: must be int (not bool) and >=32 else ValueError. Store.\n\nformat: must apply argument substitution first: record.getMessage(). But careful: super().format(record) sets record.message = record.getMessage(). Approach:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        msg = super().format(record)  # sets record.message\n        ...\n```\nWe must not modify record.msg/args. record.message is derived; modifying record.message is fine? Task says cannot rewrite msg,args,exc_info,stack_info. record.message is set by Formatter.format. But if other handlers use same record... logging.Formatter.format sets record.message = record.getMessage(); another handler's format would reset it. Safer: compute truncated message locally and build output ourselves? But we must preserve multi-line prefix, time precision, colors, exception handling.\n\nTrick: compute truncated message, then temporarily... Better approach: create a shallow copy of record with truncated msg? Use copy? copy not... `copy` module is not in imports list, but we can use `logging.makeLogRecord`? Simplest: use a proxy:\n\nActually can do: compute full message via record.getMessage(); if budget and not keep_full: truncated. Then build formatted string by temporarily setting record.message and record.exc_text? Formatter.format uses record.message for the main message and appends exception text using record.exc_info. If we set record.message = truncated, exception appended still fine. Then after super().format, restore record.message to full. But the record passed by handler chain: handler calls formatter.format(record) for one handler at a time; concurrency isn't an issue typically. But restoring is needed so subsequent handler gets full. Let's do:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        truncated = self._budgeted(message) if ... else message\n        if truncated is message:\n            msg = super().format(record)\n        else:\n            original = getattr(record, \"message\", None)  # record.message not set yet\n            record.message = truncated\n            try:\n                msg = super().format(record)\n            finally:\n                record.message = message\n```\nHmm super().format sets record.message = record.getMessage() again at start? In CPython 3.12, Formatter.format: `record.message = record.getMessage()`. Yes it does reassign. So we need to set record.msg? Not allowed. Alternative: call the parent's internals manually. Actually simpler: compute truncated as replacement of record.message within the formatted output? No—prefix may contain similar text.\n\nAlternative approach: temporarily swap record.msg and args? Not allowed to rewrite—temporarily changing then restoring... risky and disallowed conceptually.\n\nBetter: build output directly emulating Formatter.format:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        record.message = record.getMessage()   # standard\n        message = record.message\n        if budget...:\n            message = truncate(message)\n        # build\n        if self.usesTime():\n            record.asctime = self.formatTime(record, self.datefmt)\n        s = self.formatMessage(record)  # uses record.message\n```\nBut formatMessage uses record.message attribute. So set record.message = truncated, call self.formatMessage(record), then restore record.message = full message. That's exactly what default does, and record.message restored afterwards. Actually restoring to full message after super().format is harmless/beneficial.\n\nSimplest implementation:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        full_message = record.getMessage()\n        record.message = full_message\n        message = full_message\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n        record.message = message\n        try:\n            msg = super().format(record)\n        finally:\n            record.message = full_message\n        ...\n```\nBut super().format reassigns record.message = record.getMessage() = full message, ignoring our truncated. So no.\n\nSo implement own:\n\n```python\n        record.message = message\n        try:\n            if self.usesTime():\n                record.asctime = self.formatTime(record, self.datefmt)\n            s = self.formatMessage(record)\n            if record.exc_info:\n                if not record.exc_text:\n                    record.exc_text = self.formatException(record.exc_info)\n            if record.exc_text:\n                if s[-1:] != \"\\n\":\n                    s = s + \"\\n\"\n                s = s + record.exc_text\n            if record.stack_info:\n                if s[-1:] != \"\\n\":\n                    s = s + \"\\n\"\n                s = s + self.formatStack(record.stack_info)\n        finally:\n            record.message = full_message\n```\nThat replicates stdlib. Then apply newline prefix logic and color.\n\nHmm but \"沿用原有多行前缀、时间精度、颜色及异常处理\" — yes.\n\nAlternatively call super().format but temporarily patch formatMessage? Could use:\n\n```python\n        original_format_message = self.formatMessage\n```\nNo, simpler to replicate. But risk: stdlib version differences. Python version here likely 3.12/3.13. Let me check the Formatter.format source in this env to replicate exactly.\n\nActually another approach that reuses super().format: temporarily set record.message via a subclass trick... We could create a proxy record using `logging.LogRecord` copy: `copy.copy` not in imports; but we can use `_log_record` class? Hmm.\n\nAlternative clean approach: use a lightweight wrapper object copying attributes:\n\n```python\nclass _RecordProxy: ...\n```\nNot needed. Replicating is fine; I'll check stdlib source.\n\nTruncate function:\n\n```python\n_MESSAGE_OMITTED = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\ndef _truncate_message(message, budget):\n    if len(message) <= budget:\n        return message\n    marker = _MESSAGE_OMITTED\n    remaining = budget - len(marker)\n    if remaining <= 0:\n        return marker[:budget]\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return message[:head] + marker + (message[len(message) - tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\nExample budget 32: marker len 15, remaining 17, head 9, tail 8. ✓\n\nBut note: exception text should not count. Since message excludes exc text. Good.\n\nAlso `stack_info` not counted.\n\nBudget check on message length measured after args substituted — record.getMessage(). Good.\n\n_setup_logger: pass max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS from deepinfer.environ. Need import: `from deepinfer.environ import envs` — check circular import: environ imports? let me check environ imports.\n\nAlso \"重复初始化不能重复添加处理器\" — existing code already handles.\n\nAlso whether init should read envs inside _setup_logger... yes.\n\nstats_logger: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nNow let's check tests to ensure exact error type details and import path. Let me read the test files partially — they're at .course/test_logger.py etc. Reading them is allowed (not modifying). That's efficient.\n\nLet me do that and also check stdlib Formatter.format."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":8921,"completion_tokens":1952,"total_tokens":10873,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4096},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1899},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":4096,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":4825},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}